cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this study, its data, or its code, please cite it as below."
title: "Beyond Student Labels: Evaluating Prompt Sensitivity in AI-Generated Practice Problems for Multilingual Learners"
abstract: >-
  A factorial audit of whether large language models change the mathematical
  difficulty (harmful) or only the linguistic complexity (appropriate) of
  AI-generated practice problems when a student is described as an English
  language learner. Across 1,620 generations from three free, open-weight model
  families, an ELL label with a named first language shortened the written
  solution by about half a step (ELL-Mandarin -0.65 steps and -4.24 operations,
  both Holm-corrected p < .001; ELL-Spanish -0.52 steps, corrected p = .007),
  while an explicit statement that the student was struggling with the topic
  moved the same measures by -0.01 (p = .91). A generic "multilingual" label was
  null and passed an equivalence test. Models also switched language unprompted
  (50.4% of ELL-Spanish problems returned in Spanish) and produced wrong answer
  keys at rates differing sharply by family (0.4% to 38.5%). Three bounds are
  reported in the paper: the measures are read from the model's own solution and
  so index solution length rather than intrinsic difficulty, the effect shrinks
  by half or more among problems whose answer key is correct, and the ELL-Spanish
  effect cannot be separated from language switching.
